const POOL=[
 {role:'student',game:'Celeste',genre:'Platformer',text:'For my analysis I looked at Celeste and its Assist Mode. The game lets you slow game speed, add air dashes or turn on invincibility, and every option is off by default. I turned game speed down to 80% for two chapters and felt a little guilty, but I still felt like I had beaten the levels myself. The menu tells you the game is meant to be played without assists, which framed the choice as a compromise. Friends on Discord said they never opened the menu because it felt like admitting defeat.'},
 {role:'student',game:'艾尔登法环',genre:'动作 RPG',text:'我选的是《艾尔登法环》的骨灰系统。召唤是可选的，但不管用不用，Boss 的血量和攻击性都一样，所以战斗明显是按"会用骨灰"调的。用骨灰把 Boss 打得很轻松，但很空虚，我没觉得自己学会了这个 Boss。一个攻略视频告诉我哪个骨灰能逃课哪个 Boss，之后就再也回不去了。我觉得开发者本意是把骨灰当帮手，玩家却把它当成作弊码。'},
 {role:'student',game:'Hollow Knight',genre:'Metroidvania',text:'My analysis is about how Hollow Knight hides its difficulty choices. I got stuck at a boss for an evening and only learned from a wiki that I could have explored a different area first and come back stronger. Nothing in the game told me leaving was an option, so it felt like a wall rather than a choice. Once I knew, the whole map felt like a difficulty menu. I loved the design in hindsight but resented it at the time.'},
 {role:'student',game:'Hades',genre:'Roguelite',text:'I analysed God Mode in Hades. It adds 2% damage resistance every time you die, it is optional, and the option text explains exactly what it does. Because the game is honest about it, using it never felt like cheating to me. It also let me keep experiencing the story, which is what I play Hades for. The community mostly treats it as a normal setting rather than a shameful one.'},
 {role:'student',game:'蔚蓝',genre:'平台跳跃',text:'我分析的是《蔚蓝》的辅助模式。游戏允许你降低游戏速度、增加冲刺次数或者开无敌，所有选项默认关闭。我把速度调到 80% 打了两章，有一点愧疚，但还是觉得关卡是自己过的。菜单上写着游戏本意是不用辅助游玩，这让选择变成了一种妥协。群里的朋友说他们从来没打开过那个菜单，因为感觉像认输。'},
 {role:'student',game:'Dark Souls',genre:'Action RPG',text:'For Dark Souls I focused on what you lose when you die rather than how hard the enemies are. Dying drops your souls where you fell and a second death loses them for good. The runback to the boss is the real penalty, and players have built routines, shortcuts and even mods to shrink it. I felt the design intended that runback as tension, but the community negotiated it down to a chore.'},
 {role:'student',game:'My capstone prototype',genre:'Puzzle platformer',text:'This analysis is about my own capstone game. Playtesters said it was too hard, so with two weeks left we added an easy mode that halves enemy damage. It worked in the sense that fewer people quit, but testers on easy said the levels felt empty, because our puzzles were built around the original damage. We did not have time to retune the levels for the new mode. If we had planned the mode from the start it would have been a different game.'},
 {role:'student',game:'Slay the Spire',genre:'Deckbuilder',text:'I chose the Ascension system in Slay the Spire. It is a ladder of twenty difficulty steps you unlock one at a time, and the game shows exactly what each step changes. Because the ladder is explicit and earned, raising difficulty felt like progress rather than a setting. Community tier lists tell you which characters can handle which ascension, and I followed them, which changed how I built decks. It is difficulty as a goal instead of difficulty as a toggle.'}
];
